    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "23e1b301_3c6c7d99",
        "filename": "src/sksl/codegen/SkSLWGSLCodeGenerator.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 999,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-17T21:18:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is possible when a variable in global scope is used as an out-param argument (imagine passing a module-private variable or, e.g., `sk_FragColor`). This is allowed by the front-end and seems to hit this exact code path.\n\nI described what I expect the final transformations to look like at skbug.com/14082, in which I actually expect the out-param helpers to go away entirely but the pointer dereferencing logic will stay around.\n\nP.S.:\nThe generated code for these doesn\u0027t actually work due to the reasons I outlined in skbug.com/14082 and because taking the address of a component is actually not allowed, s.t. the following code is not valid WGSL:\n\n    fn _outParamHelper_vec4(_var0: ptr\u003cfunction, vec4\u003cf32\u003e\u003e) {...}\n    \n    fn main(_stageOut: ptr\u003cfunction, FSOut\u003e) {\n        _outParamHelper_vec4(\u0026(*_stageOut).sk_FragColor);\n    }\n\nwhich generates `error: :102:42 error: arguments of pointer type must not point to a subset of the originating variable` (this is the \"taking the address of a struct component\" issue that I mentioned in the commit message).\n\nThat\u0027s why I left TODOs to write test cases for this situation after skbug.com/14082 is addressed. For now, this CL leaves the out-param handling in a partially functioning state while still introducing some tests for the trivial cases.\n\nOnce I address skbug.com/14082, I expect all out-param arguments to always be local variables only, at which point we can safely assert `!fOutParamArgVars.contains`.",
      "parentUuid": "d37e76cb_42a355f6",
